<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Prespa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Prespa is a pair of lakes in the mountains of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, shared by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, Albania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Around 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Samuel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> made it a centre of his realm, building a basilica and residence on the island of Saint Achillius in Small Prespa Lake, where relics of the saint were brought from Larissa. The area remained a seat of Bulgarian power until the Byzantine conquest of 1018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-prespa.jpg" alt="Lake Prespa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fation Plaku · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liqeni_Prespes._Ishulli_Mali_Gradit._Korce._Albania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
